<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christian III of Denmark</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1503–1559</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christian III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> was king of Denmark and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1534 to 1559. He won the throne in the civil war known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counts-feud">Count's Feud</a> and in 1536 introduced the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, seizing the lands of the church and strengthening the crown. In 1537 he declared Norway a province of the Danish crown, though it kept its own laws.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christian-iii-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Christian III of Denmark" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacob Binck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christian_III_of_Denmark.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
